274:- scene: Three numbered rules on the left; the real register sheet on the right with an accent box on "6 sealed · 0 scored".
275:- voiceover: "That is why forecasts now go on the record. The register picks the three most active fires on each island by a fixed rule, applied automatically at issue time. Nobody picks the easy fires. Each gets a live scenario, a twenty-four hour forecast, and a seal."
276:- poster: 8s
277:- audio: audio/scene-06-register-rule.aiff
307:## Frame 7 — The seal: hash, signature, Bitcoin
309:- status: outline
310:- src: compositions/07-seal-bitcoin.html
311:- duration: 29s
312:- start: 139.0s
313:- transition_in: cut
314:- scene: Three sealing steps, then a typeset terminal showing the reference `ots` client verifying a real Bitcoin transaction.
315:- voiceover: "Sealing is three things. A SHA-256 hash over every input and every simulated cell. An Ed25519 signature over that hash. And an OpenTimestamps anchor committed to Bitcoin. Six forecasts were sealed on the twenty-first of October — three in Kalimantan, three in Sumatra — before their outcome existed. Twenty-four hours later each one is scored against NASA FIRMS, and stays on the board, good or bad. Here is the reference client verifying one: a real Bitcoin transaction, still waiting for confirmations."
316:- poster: 20s
317:- audio: audio/scene-07-seal-bitcoin.aiff
376:## Frame 8 — Why Bitcoin: a public clock
378:- status: outline
379:- src: compositions/08-why-bitcoin.html
380:- duration: 21s
381:- start: 168.0s
382:- transition_in: crossfade
383:- scene: The argument on the left, the real Record panel on the right with the four green checks outlined.
384:- voiceover: "Bitcoin here is a public clock and nothing more. One hash per forecast. No token, no wallet, no fee. A smart contract cannot make a fire model more correct — a deterministic re-run can. So the record shows four things: the receipt is unchanged, the inputs are unchanged, the signature is valid, and re-running gives the same result."
385:- poster: 12s
