Both files are written and internally consistent.

**`video/SCRIPT.md`** — 10 narration lines in the HyperFrames `SCRIPT.md` shape (header with voice/settings/direction, then `## Line N — label (Frame N)` with Time, Delivery, and the indented spoken block). **664 words / 244 s (4:04) = 163 wpm**, inside the 3:30–4:15 window and the 580–700 word band. Numbers are spelled out so `say` paces predictably; the register's forecasts are never given a score.

**`video/STORYBOARD.md`** — frontmatter + one `## Frame N — Title` block per scene, each with `status/src/duration/start/transition_in/scene/voiceover/poster/audio`, then the exact narration quote, the visuals (asset file, source-rect pixel crops, placement, Ken Burns camera spec, accent-outline callouts with their literal text, and the charts/terminal to build with their exact figures), timed caption cards (≤2 lines each), and the transition out. Durations sum to 244 s with no gaps or overlaps, and every `voiceover` string matches its `SCRIPT.md` line verbatim.

The **Fact check** table lists 63 sourced claims with BRIEF.md line numbers or `data.json` JSON paths, plus an explicit "removed for lack of a source" list — the register's `detections + FRP/50` formula, any score for the six pending forecasts, "avoided emissions"/credits/offsets, safety or agency-deployment claims, and "AI predicts".

Two things I changed from my first draft, both for fact-check integrity: "forecasts that steer crews and aircraft" and "provincial governments deciding when to request aircraft" are not in BRIEF.md, so I trimmed them to what line 25 and line 32 actually support and re-timed the whole film to keep every scene at ~165 wpm.
